[38;5;231m⏺[39m Section 4 of 4: [1mtesting.[0m

  Node here is v26, so [38;5;153mnode:test[39m and [38;5;153mnode:assert[39m are built in — a real runner with zero dependencies, which keeps
  [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m dependency-free as it is today. It gains only a scripts block: [38;5;153m"test": "node --test"[39m. Tests land in
  [38;5;153mtest/prefs.test.js[39m.

  The injected storage seam does the heavy lifting: a ~10-line [38;5;153mMap[39m-backed fake implementing [38;5;153mgetItem[39m/[38;5;153msetItem[39m/[38;5;153mremoveItem[39m
  stands in for [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, so no jsdom and no browser. Written test-first, per TDD.

  Coverage:

  - Empty storage returns registry defaults for both preferences
  - [38;5;153mset[39m then [38;5;153mget[39m round-trips each preference
  - [1mA fresh [38;5;153mcreatePrefs[39m over the same storage sees prior values[0m — this is the actual "persists across sessions" claim,
    and it's the one test that would catch a store that only ever worked in-memory
  - Corrupt JSON in the key falls back to all defaults
  - An invalid [38;5;153mtheme[39m falls back to its default [3mwhile a valid sibling username survives[0m — the per-field guarantee from
    section 3
  - An unrecognized [38;5;153mv[39m falls back to defaults
  - [38;5;153mget[39m/[38;5;153mset[39m on an unknown key throws; [38;5;153mset[39m with an invalid value throws
  - [38;5;153mclear()[39m restores defaults
  - [38;5;153mset[39m returns [38;5;153mfalse[39m when the backend throws on write, instead of propagating
  - A backend that throws on access yields a working in-memory store
  - [38;5;153m"password"[39m is not a registered key, so [38;5;153mset("password", …)[39m throws — the security constraint from section 2 as an
    executable assertion rather than a comment

  What this deliberately does not cover: the DOM wiring in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — theme class application, field pre-fill, the
  checkbox branch. Testing that needs jsdom or Playwright, both of which you ruled out. That code stays thin and
  verified by hand precisely because it's untested; all the branching logic sits in [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m where the tests are. Worth
  knowing that's the gap rather than discovering it later.

